---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../content';
const title = 'Shopify email tools: choose by the work your store needs';
const desc = 'Find a route through Shopify email tools, workflow guides, store contexts and comparisons. Start with the job, data needs and operating constraints.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path="/">
  <section class="container py-16">
    <p class="catalog-kicker">Shopify email tools</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Choose email tools around the work your store needs</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl">A campaign editor, a customer journey builder and a stock-alert service solve different problems. Start with the task that is failing today, then decide which capabilities belong in your sender and which need another layer.</p>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Browse the app catalog</a><a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/use-cases">Find your email job</a></div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16">
    <article class="card prose-shopify">
      <h2>Choose a starting route</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/apps">App profiles</a>: investigate a named product, including Sequenzy, Shopify's email offering or a candidate already in your stack. Use current supplier documentation and a written quote to confirm capabilities and costs before buying.</li>
        <li><a href="/use-cases">Use-case guides</a>: define a workflow before shopping. Start with <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">checkout recovery</a>, <a href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">restock alerts</a> or <a href="/use-cases/analytics">email analytics</a> when that is the bottleneck.</li>
        <li><a href="/for">Store contexts</a>: identify catalog constraints such as variants, replenishment, long consideration or wholesale terms.</li>
        <li><a href="/compare">Pairwise comparisons</a> and <a href="/alternatives">alternatives</a>: explore candidates when a renewal, missing workflow or migration makes a change worth considering.</li>
        <li><a href="/guides">Implementation guides</a>: plan data, consent, measurement and cutover responsibilities.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These routes help organize investigation; a listing or its position is not proof that an app meets your requirements. Product plans, connectors and features need confirmation for the configuration you intend to use.</p>
      <h2>Separate the layers before adding another subscription</h2>
      <p><strong>Capture</strong> collects an address and a stated interest. <strong>Sending</strong> handles content, audience and delivery. <strong>Automation</strong> reacts to customer events and rechecks eligibility. <strong>Measurement</strong> connects exposure to orders and costs. A single platform may cover several layers, but draw the boundaries before assuming a missing feature requires replacing everything.</p>
      <p>For a founder producing occasional newsletters, draft a product launch, update a price and remove an unavailable variant in a demonstration. Measure the editing and approval work. For a retention operator, trace one customer from checkout through purchase and a delayed reminder; inspect exits and competing journeys. A polished campaign demo does not answer that second question.</p>
      <h2>Let catalog behavior change the requirements</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fashion and limited editions:</strong> distinguish product from variant interest. A size-specific waitlist should not become a promise that every size has returned.</li>
        <li><strong>Consumables:</strong> distinguish an estimated usage cycle from a subscription renewal. A reorder promotion should not contradict an active recurring order.</li>
        <li><strong>Jewelry and considered purchases:</strong> make room for sizing, materials, delivery deadlines and consultation. More urgency cannot resolve an unanswered product question.</li>
        <li><strong>Home goods:</strong> collect useful category or style preferences without treating a single page view as a durable preference.</li>
        <li><strong>Wholesale:</strong> keep account terms, rep ownership and reorder communications separate from consumer sale offers.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>A shortlist record your team can use</h2>
      <p>Write the current problem, required fields, unacceptable failure and responsible owner. Classify requirements as mandatory or optional before scoring candidates. A missing mandatory exit rule should not be averaged away by attractive templates. The <a href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> expands this buying record.</p>
      <p>Ask each finalist to demonstrate the same proposed scenario in its supported Shopify integration. Include a changed order, unavailable item and withdrawn marketing permission. Record which behavior is native, configured, custom or unsupported. These are proposed acceptance checks, not tests performed by this catalog.</p>
      <p>Request costs for the same workload: stored and billable profiles, email volume, additional channels, required modules, support, implementation and overages. Include seasonal peaks and the cost of keeping an old sender during migration. Avoid comparing a starter tier against a quote with different features.</p>
      <h2>Sequence implementation by dependencies</h2>
      <p>First inventory forms, senders, live journeys and identity rules. Next define ownership of consent and suppression. Then rebuild one workflow and verify its entry, content, exits and reporting before adding others. At cutover, assign one active sender per journey and retain a rollback record. Use the <a href="/guides/migration">migration guide</a> rather than treating DNS, templates and customer history as independent switches.</p>
      <p>After launch, review customer problems alongside delivery and commercial metrics. Reconcile revenue definitions before calling a new tool successful: attributed sales and incremental sales answer different questions. The <a href="/guides/revenue-attribution">revenue attribution guide</a> covers that distinction. A tool earns its place through reliable operation and useful decisions, not through a larger dashboard total alone.</p>
      <h2>Where Shopify's native tools fit</h2>
      <p>Do not assume you need another platform for checkout recovery. Shopify documents an <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/migrate-abandoned-checkout">abandoned-checkout automation in Shopify Messaging</a>. Compare the supported workflow with your actual requirements before deciding to keep it, extend it or replace it. That documentation does not establish equivalent functionality in every catalog app.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
